N1  - Wiley Online Books
N2  - Summary Due to the increasing length and complexity of space missions, pressure is growing to find a more flexible, quick, and sure technique of manufacturing electronic parts in space conditions. This chapter examines how the 3D printing and additive manufacturing technologies can be used to enable on-demand production of space electronics. It describes different printing methods including inkjet, aerosol jet, and fused deposition modeling (FDM) and the choice of materials that can be used in printable electronics in space like conductive inks, polymers, and metal alloys. Such issues as vacuum compatibility, thermal stress, and radiation resistance are identified, and some possible solutions are offered. Application of printed electronics in structural and functional units of satellites, landers, and lunar habitats is also discussed. Case studies and real-life examples of activities conducted by space agencies and even commercial organizations are given to demonstrate the existing capabilities and the future perspectives. This chapter ends by underlining the disruptive nature of additive manufacturing in making self-sustaining and sustainable in-space electronic fabrication systems.
