N2  - Summary Artificial intelligence (AI) and synthetic aperture radar (SAR) have transformed disaster management by providing real-time monitoring, quick decision-making, and effective resource allocation. Natural and human-made disasters threaten lives and can harm infrastructure and the environment, thus requiring the deployment of new technologies for better preparedness and response. Processing SAR data using AI improves its predictive capability, assisting in risk assessment, early warning systems, and infrastructure preparedness. Artificial intelligence also enhances community awareness and prepositioning of resources to aid in successful disaster preparedness. From a methodological perspective, AI utilizes machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) algorithms to analyze SAR data, enabling automation of feature extraction, noise elimination, change detection, and real-time prediction of disaster scenarios. Artificial intelligence can be used alongside SAR to automate disaster monitoring, optimize resource allocation, and enhance emergency response with real-time situational awareness and damage assessment. As evidenced through the outcomes, integrating AI with SAR data leads to a considerable enhancement in predicting disasters, shortening the time taken for response and optimizing the allocation of resources, which in turn mitigates the loss of life and damage to the economy. This chapter discusses SAR data analysis methods utilizing AI, particularly DL, with its convolutional neural networks for feature extraction, generative adversarial networks for image processing, and recurrent neural networks for time series disaster monitoring. Artificial intelligence is also being used in emergency coordination, including rescue missions with unmanned aerial vehicles, robotics, and improved logistics planning. While these benefits are tremendous, data privacy, AI bias, the expensive infrastructure in underdeveloped areas, and ethical issues pose challenges for AI-SAR integration and, therefore, need more evidence for implementation. However, through AI-SAR synergy, disaster resilience is becoming increasingly feasible with proactive risk assessment and mitigation, real-time response to disasters, and improved recovery after such events. In the latter case, future systems will integrate seamlessly using AI-powered SAR satellite constellations, Internet of Things (IoT) technologies, and digital twin technology to foil disasters when they occur and contribute to humanitarian efforts. The key to leveraging AI-SAR technology in effective disaster management will be to tackle ethical concerns and promote global collaboration.
